  {
    "number": 1102,
    "title": "Resume synchronisation where it fails",
    "state": "closed",
    "draft": false,
    "merged": true,
    "created_at": "2026-06-27T06:58:00Z",
    "merged_at": "2026-06-28T22:05:00Z",
    "closed_at": "2026-06-28T22:05:00Z",
    "head": {
      "ref": "feat/1102"
    },
    "base": {
      "ref": "main"
    },
    "changed_files": 11,
    "additions": 320,
    "deletions": 174,
    "commits": 6,
    "review_comments": 2,
    "body": "## The problem\n\nA network drop halfway through a 40,000-record synchronisation made the whole\nthing start over. The job runs once a night, so a failure costs a\nfull day: three nights in a row and the warehouse was three days behind.\n\n## What I change\n\nBatch processing, with retries per batch rather than on the whole run. The wait\ndoubles on each attempt — without that, three clients retrying at the same time\nfinish the network's job for it.\n\n## What I checked\n\nTests cover the batch that fails then succeeds, giving up after the last\nattempt, the empty source, and dry-run mode. I replayed the synchronisation\nfrom the night of the 12th, cutting the network halfway: correct resume, no\nduplicates in the database.\n\n## What I am not doing\n\nResuming after a process restart. That would need progress persisted, so a\ntable and a migration. It will be worth it once we go past an hour of\nprocessing — we are at 22 minutes.\n\n## Review\n\nOne remark from @marc on the name of `batchSize`. Kept as is — the option is\npublic and two integrations pass it. Reasoning in `aidd_docs/tasks/1102.md`."
  },
